1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,195
Ten years old.

2
00:00:01,375 --> 00:00:02,481
For many people,

3
00:00:02,481 --> 00:00:04,347
it's the best age there is.

4
00:00:04,527 --> 00:00:06,842
Old enough to ride your bike across

5
00:00:06,842 --> 00:00:08,892
town, but too young to have any

6
00:00:08,892 --> 00:00:09,752
real worries.

7
00:00:09,932 --> 00:00:11,786
Old enough to stay up for a

8
00:00:11,786 --> 00:00:14,122
TV show, but young enough to still

9
00:00:14,122 --> 00:00:16,457
believe the world was mostly good.

10
00:00:16,637 --> 00:00:18,627
And if you were ten years old

11
00:00:18,627 --> 00:00:20,273
in nineteen sixty-three,

12
00:00:20,273 --> 00:00:22,263
you had one of the best years

13
00:00:22,263 --> 00:00:22,744
of all.

14
00:00:23,094 --> 00:00:25,301
If you were ten in sixty-three,

15
00:00:25,301 --> 00:00:28,008
you were born in nineteen fifty-three,

16
00:00:28,008 --> 00:00:30,145
right in the heart of the baby

17
00:00:30,145 --> 00:00:30,501
boom.

18
00:00:30,681 --> 00:00:32,945
You grew up surrounded by kids.

19
00:00:33,125 --> 00:00:35,899
Your classroom had thirty-five children in it.

20
00:00:36,079 --> 00:00:37,705
Your street had dozens.

21
00:00:37,885 --> 00:00:39,029
Wherever you went,

22
00:00:39,029 --> 00:00:41,507
there was always somebody to play with.

23
00:00:41,858 --> 00:00:44,419
So today, let's look at ten things

24
00:00:44,419 --> 00:00:46,830
that made being ten years old in

25
00:00:46,830 --> 00:00:49,241
nineteen sixty-three so special.

26
00:00:49,421 --> 00:00:51,697
Ten, for ten years old.

27
00:00:51,877 --> 00:00:53,600
See how many of them were true

28
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:54,060
for you.

29
00:00:54,410 --> 00:00:56,197
The first reason was freedom.

30
00:00:56,377 --> 00:00:59,931
A ten-year-old in nineteen sixty-three could leave

31
00:00:59,931 --> 00:01:02,632
the house after breakfast and not come

32
00:01:02,632 --> 00:01:03,840
back until lunch,

33
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:04,551
or dinner.

34
00:01:04,731 --> 00:01:07,182
Mothers didn't ask exactly where you were

35
00:01:07,182 --> 00:01:07,540
going.

36
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:08,608
They just said,

37
00:01:08,608 --> 00:01:10,855
be home when the streetlights come on.

38
00:01:11,035 --> 00:01:11,825
And you were.

39
00:01:12,175 --> 00:01:14,102
Kids roamed in packs.

40
00:01:14,282 --> 00:01:16,395
They rode bikes to the creek,

41
00:01:16,395 --> 00:01:18,800
the woods, the vacant lot and the

42
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:19,820
five-and-dime.

43
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,138
They built forts out of scrap lumber

44
00:01:22,138 --> 00:01:24,277
and climbed trees that were much too

45
00:01:24,277 --> 00:01:24,574
high.

46
00:01:24,754 --> 00:01:26,293
If somebody got hurt,

47
00:01:26,293 --> 00:01:28,198
they walked home bleeding,

48
00:01:28,198 --> 00:01:30,983
got a Band-Aid and some Mercurochrome,

49
00:01:30,983 --> 00:01:32,742
and went right back out.

50
00:01:33,092 --> 00:01:35,880
Ten-year-olds also walked to school,

51
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:37,506
often a mile or more,

52
00:01:37,506 --> 00:01:38,978
without a grown-up.

53
00:01:39,158 --> 00:01:41,949
They crossed busy streets with a crossing

54
00:01:41,949 --> 00:01:43,923
guard, or a sixth grader in a

55
00:01:43,923 --> 00:01:45,625
white safety patrol belt.

56
00:01:45,805 --> 00:01:46,797
Along the way,

57
00:01:46,797 --> 00:01:48,428
they collected friends,

58
00:01:48,428 --> 00:01:50,980
kicked rocks, and stopped to look at

59
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:52,469
anything interesting.

60
00:01:52,819 --> 00:01:56,336
And ten-year-olds had a lot of responsibility.

61
00:01:56,516 --> 00:01:59,094
They watched their little brothers and sisters.

62
00:01:59,274 --> 00:02:01,547
They walked to the corner store with

63
00:02:01,547 --> 00:02:03,441
a note from their mother and a

64
00:02:03,441 --> 00:02:05,777
few dollars, and came back with milk,

65
00:02:05,777 --> 00:02:07,482
bread and the right change.

66
00:02:07,662 --> 00:02:09,217
They took out the trash,

67
00:02:09,217 --> 00:02:11,226
fed the dog and mowed the lawn.

68
00:02:11,406 --> 00:02:14,064
Nobody thought of a ten-year-old as a

69
00:02:14,064 --> 00:02:14,854
little kid.

70
00:02:15,204 --> 00:02:17,643
The second reason was outer space.

71
00:02:17,823 --> 00:02:20,950
For a ten-year-old in nineteen sixty-three,

72
00:02:20,950 --> 00:02:24,004
astronauts were the greatest heroes in the

73
00:02:24,004 --> 00:02:24,440
world.

74
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:27,212
John Glenn had orbited the Earth in

75
00:02:27,212 --> 00:02:29,508
February of nineteen sixty-two.

76
00:02:29,688 --> 00:02:31,411
In May of sixty-three,

77
00:02:31,411 --> 00:02:34,074
Gordon Cooper flew for more than a

78
00:02:34,074 --> 00:02:35,562
day in Faith Seven,

79
00:02:35,562 --> 00:02:38,303
the last flight of Project Mercury.

80
00:02:38,652 --> 00:02:42,559
Schools wheeled televisions into classrooms so children

81
00:02:42,559 --> 00:02:44,690
could watch the launches live.

82
00:02:44,870 --> 00:02:47,424
Teachers counted down with the announcer.

83
00:02:47,604 --> 00:02:49,829
And every child dreamed of going to

84
00:02:49,829 --> 00:02:52,880
the moon, because President Kennedy had promised

85
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:55,550
that Americans would land there before the

86
00:02:55,550 --> 00:02:56,694
end of the decade.

87
00:02:56,874 --> 00:03:00,045
A ten-year-old in sixty-three would be sixteen

88
00:03:00,045 --> 00:03:01,217
when it happened.

89
00:03:01,566 --> 00:03:02,986
Kids drank Tang,

90
00:03:02,986 --> 00:03:05,026
because astronauts did.

91
00:03:05,206 --> 00:03:07,099
They built model rockets.

92
00:03:07,279 --> 00:03:10,021
They collected astronaut trading cards,

93
00:03:10,021 --> 00:03:12,975
and wore space helmets made from cardboard

94
00:03:12,975 --> 00:03:13,397
boxes.

95
00:03:13,577 --> 00:03:16,203
Many of the scientists and engineers of

96
00:03:16,203 --> 00:03:18,828
the next generation said they got their

97
00:03:18,828 --> 00:03:19,703
start at ten,

98
00:03:19,703 --> 00:03:22,261
watching those rockets on a school TV.

99
00:03:22,611 --> 00:03:24,806
The third reason was television.

100
00:03:24,986 --> 00:03:26,988
In nineteen sixty-three,

101
00:03:26,988 --> 00:03:29,990
more than nine in ten American homes

102
00:03:29,990 --> 00:03:30,741
had a TV,

103
00:03:30,741 --> 00:03:33,160
and kids had some of the best

104
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:34,494
shows ever made.

105
00:03:34,674 --> 00:03:36,300
The Andy Griffith Show.

106
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:38,035
The Dick Van Dyke Show.

107
00:03:38,215 --> 00:03:39,832
The Beverly Hillbillies,

108
00:03:39,832 --> 00:03:42,123
which was the most popular show in

109
00:03:42,123 --> 00:03:42,662
America.

110
00:03:42,842 --> 00:03:44,320
And Leave It to Beaver,

111
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:45,605
in its final season.

112
00:03:45,955 --> 00:03:48,284
There was The Flintstones and The Jetsons

113
00:03:48,284 --> 00:03:49,079
in prime time.

114
00:03:49,259 --> 00:03:52,382
Lassie and Walt Disney on Sunday nights.

115
00:03:52,562 --> 00:03:55,241
Bonanza, Gunsmoke and The Fugitive,

116
00:03:55,241 --> 00:03:57,078
which started that fall.

117
00:03:57,258 --> 00:03:59,359
And on Saturday mornings,

118
00:03:59,359 --> 00:04:02,552
Bugs Bunny, Mighty Mouse and Sky King.

119
00:04:02,732 --> 00:04:05,126
Almost all of it was still in

120
00:04:05,126 --> 00:04:06,447
black and white.

121
00:04:06,797 --> 00:04:10,133
Ten-year-olds also had local kids' shows,

122
00:04:10,133 --> 00:04:12,168
with a host in a costume,

123
00:04:12,168 --> 00:04:14,609
a studio audience of children,

124
00:04:14,609 --> 00:04:16,561
and cartoons in between.

125
00:04:16,741 --> 00:04:18,373
Every city had one,

126
00:04:18,373 --> 00:04:19,489
with a clown,

127
00:04:19,489 --> 00:04:21,722
a cowboy or a sea captain.

128
00:04:21,902 --> 00:04:24,152
Kids waited months for a chance to

129
00:04:24,152 --> 00:04:25,409
be in the audience,

130
00:04:25,409 --> 00:04:27,857
and wore their best clothes when they

131
00:04:27,857 --> 00:04:28,717
finally went.

132
00:04:29,067 --> 00:04:30,449
And there was the radio.

133
00:04:30,629 --> 00:04:34,320
At night, ten-year-olds hid transistor radios under

134
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:37,360
their pillows, listening to ball games and

135
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:40,255
rock and roll stations long after lights

136
00:04:40,255 --> 00:04:40,544
out.

137
00:04:40,724 --> 00:04:42,072
On summer evenings,

138
00:04:42,072 --> 00:04:44,626
the sound of a baseball game drifted

139
00:04:44,626 --> 00:04:45,832
from every porch.

140
00:04:46,012 --> 00:04:48,745
Many people still associate the voice of

141
00:04:48,745 --> 00:04:51,683
their team's announcer with being ten years

142
00:04:51,683 --> 00:04:51,957
old.

143
00:04:52,306 --> 00:04:54,768
The fourth reason was the toys.

144
00:04:54,948 --> 00:04:56,794
In nineteen sixty-three,

145
00:04:56,794 --> 00:04:58,025
kids had Barbie,

146
00:04:58,025 --> 00:04:59,256
Ken and Skipper,

147
00:04:59,256 --> 00:05:02,180
the Easy-Bake Oven which came out that

148
00:05:02,180 --> 00:05:04,027
year, and the Hula Hoop.

149
00:05:04,207 --> 00:05:05,951
Boys had cap guns,

150
00:05:05,951 --> 00:05:10,604
Matchbox cars, Erector sets and electric trains.

151
00:05:10,784 --> 00:05:12,638
There were Slinkys,

152
00:05:12,638 --> 00:05:15,857
yo-yos, kites, marbles and jacks.

153
00:05:16,207 --> 00:05:18,365
The most exciting toy of all might

154
00:05:18,365 --> 00:05:19,888
have been a new bicycle.

155
00:05:20,068 --> 00:05:21,831
A Schwinn, with a bell,

156
00:05:21,831 --> 00:05:23,748
a basket and a headlight.

157
00:05:23,928 --> 00:05:26,007
Or a pair of metal roller skates,

158
00:05:26,007 --> 00:05:28,401
tightened onto your shoes with a skate

159
00:05:28,401 --> 00:05:30,417
key worn on a string around your

160
00:05:30,417 --> 00:05:30,732
neck.

161
00:05:30,912 --> 00:05:32,429
Kids skated for miles,

162
00:05:32,429 --> 00:05:35,672
and the clattering sound filled every sidewalk.

163
00:05:36,021 --> 00:05:37,152
And best of all,

164
00:05:37,152 --> 00:05:39,271
the Sears Christmas Wish Book,

165
00:05:39,271 --> 00:05:41,037
which arrived every fall.

166
00:05:41,217 --> 00:05:43,794
A ten-year-old studied it like a scholar.

167
00:05:43,974 --> 00:05:45,774
Every page was memorized.

168
00:05:45,954 --> 00:05:47,533
Every toy was circled.

169
00:05:47,713 --> 00:05:48,684
And every night,

170
00:05:48,684 --> 00:05:50,989
they dreamed about what might be under

171
00:05:50,989 --> 00:05:52,810
the tree on Christmas morning.

172
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:56,898
Many ten-year-olds also joined the Cub Scouts,

173
00:05:56,898 --> 00:05:58,848
Brownies or Girl Scouts.

174
00:05:59,028 --> 00:06:01,797
They wore uniforms to school on meeting

175
00:06:01,797 --> 00:06:03,216
days, earned badges,

176
00:06:03,216 --> 00:06:05,061
sold cookies door to door,

177
00:06:05,061 --> 00:06:07,475
and went on camping trips where it

178
00:06:07,475 --> 00:06:09,036
always seemed to rain.

179
00:06:09,216 --> 00:06:10,379
The pinewood derby,

180
00:06:10,379 --> 00:06:12,765
where boys carved little race cars from

181
00:06:12,765 --> 00:06:14,846
blocks of wood with their fathers,

182
00:06:14,846 --> 00:06:16,681
was the highlight of the year.

183
00:06:17,032 --> 00:06:19,039
The fifth reason was school,

184
00:06:19,039 --> 00:06:20,329
believe it or not.

185
00:06:20,509 --> 00:06:23,706
Fourth and fifth grade in nineteen sixty-three

186
00:06:23,706 --> 00:06:25,652
meant reading Dick and Jane,

187
00:06:25,652 --> 00:06:28,363
practicing cursive with a fountain pen,

188
00:06:28,363 --> 00:06:31,143
and learning your times tables by heart.

189
00:06:31,323 --> 00:06:32,710
It meant milk breaks,

190
00:06:32,710 --> 00:06:34,955
recess, and a teacher who seemed a

191
00:06:34,955 --> 00:06:37,663
hundred years old but was probably forty.

192
00:06:38,013 --> 00:06:40,735
At recess, kids played kickball,

193
00:06:40,735 --> 00:06:43,968
four square, tetherball and jump rope.

194
00:06:44,148 --> 00:06:46,528
Girls sang rhymes while they jumped.

195
00:06:46,708 --> 00:06:50,121
Boys traded baseball cards and argued about

196
00:06:50,121 --> 00:06:52,502
Mickey Mantle and Willie Mays.

197
00:06:52,682 --> 00:06:55,442
The playground had metal slides that got

198
00:06:55,442 --> 00:06:56,960
scorching hot in June,

199
00:06:56,960 --> 00:06:59,996
and merry-go-rounds that spun much too fast.

200
00:07:00,347 --> 00:07:02,769
Lunch at school cost about a quarter,

201
00:07:02,769 --> 00:07:04,471
and it came on a tray with

202
00:07:04,471 --> 00:07:05,780
little compartments.

203
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:08,182
Fish sticks, sloppy joes,

204
00:07:08,182 --> 00:07:11,115
pizza squares and canned peaches.

205
00:07:11,295 --> 00:07:13,361
Or kids brought a lunch box with

206
00:07:13,361 --> 00:07:14,394
a cartoon on it,

207
00:07:14,394 --> 00:07:16,589
and a thermos that usually leaked.

208
00:07:16,769 --> 00:07:19,303
Trading at the lunch table was serious

209
00:07:19,303 --> 00:07:19,904
business.

210
00:07:20,084 --> 00:07:23,079
A homemade cookie could buy almost anything.

211
00:07:23,429 --> 00:07:25,170
And there were the school drills.

212
00:07:25,350 --> 00:07:28,579
Fire drills, and in nineteen sixty-three,

213
00:07:28,579 --> 00:07:31,886
still the occasional duck-and-cover drill.

214
00:07:32,066 --> 00:07:34,972
The Cuban Missile Crisis had happened just

215
00:07:34,972 --> 00:07:35,941
a year before,

216
00:07:35,941 --> 00:07:37,601
in October of sixty-two,

217
00:07:37,601 --> 00:07:41,061
and many ten-year-olds remembered how scared their

218
00:07:41,061 --> 00:07:42,237
parents had been.

219
00:07:42,417 --> 00:07:44,760
But most kids treated the drills like

220
00:07:44,760 --> 00:07:45,203
a game.

221
00:07:45,554 --> 00:07:47,655
The sixth reason was summer.

222
00:07:47,835 --> 00:07:49,775
School let out in early June,

223
00:07:49,775 --> 00:07:52,049
and summer seemed to last forever.

224
00:07:52,229 --> 00:07:54,640
There was the public swimming pool,

225
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:56,431
with a deep end and a high

226
00:07:56,431 --> 00:07:57,326
diving board.

227
00:07:57,506 --> 00:07:58,656
There was Little League.

228
00:07:58,836 --> 00:08:00,483
There was summer camp,

229
00:08:00,483 --> 00:08:03,403
Vacation Bible School and long trips to

230
00:08:03,403 --> 00:08:04,675
see grandparents.

231
00:08:05,026 --> 00:08:06,186
On summer nights,

232
00:08:06,186 --> 00:08:08,915
kids caught fireflies in mason jars with

233
00:08:08,915 --> 00:08:10,622
holes punched in the lid.

234
00:08:10,802 --> 00:08:12,714
They played kick the can,

235
00:08:12,714 --> 00:08:15,239
red rover and freeze tag until it

236
00:08:15,239 --> 00:08:16,769
was too dark to see.

237
00:08:16,949 --> 00:08:18,933
They chased the ice cream truck,

238
00:08:18,933 --> 00:08:20,793
and the mosquito fogger truck,

239
00:08:20,793 --> 00:08:22,777
which they should not have done.

240
00:08:22,957 --> 00:08:24,940
And they fell asleep exhausted,

241
00:08:24,940 --> 00:08:26,347
with the windows open.

242
00:08:26,698 --> 00:08:29,180
And in the summer of sixty-three,

243
00:08:29,180 --> 00:08:31,887
many towns held fairs and carnivals.

244
00:08:32,067 --> 00:08:34,470
Ferris wheels, cotton candy,

245
00:08:34,470 --> 00:08:37,560
the smell of popcorn and diesel from

246
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:38,418
the rides.

247
00:08:38,598 --> 00:08:41,806
Kids rode the Tilt-A-Whirl until they felt

248
00:08:41,806 --> 00:08:44,250
sick, tried to win a goldfish by

249
00:08:44,250 --> 00:08:47,077
tossing a ping-pong ball into a bowl,

250
00:08:47,077 --> 00:08:49,674
and came home sunburned and happy.

251
00:08:50,024 --> 00:08:51,962
The seventh reason was music.

252
00:08:52,142 --> 00:08:54,212
In nineteen sixty-three,

253
00:08:54,212 --> 00:08:56,972
the radio was full of fun songs.

254
00:08:57,152 --> 00:08:59,114
The Beach Boys sang Surfin' U.

255
00:08:59,294 --> 00:08:59,677
S.

256
00:08:59,857 --> 00:09:00,066
A.

257
00:09:00,246 --> 00:09:01,740
Little Stevie Wonder,

258
00:09:01,740 --> 00:09:03,304
who was just thirteen,

259
00:09:03,304 --> 00:09:05,935
had a number one hit with Fingertips.

260
00:09:06,115 --> 00:09:08,333
There was It's My Party by Lesley

261
00:09:08,333 --> 00:09:10,282
Gore, and He's So Fine by the

262
00:09:10,282 --> 00:09:10,887
Chiffons.

263
00:09:11,237 --> 00:09:12,942
And on the other side of the

264
00:09:12,942 --> 00:09:15,196
ocean, something was about to happen.

265
00:09:15,376 --> 00:09:17,084
In nineteen sixty-three,

266
00:09:17,084 --> 00:09:20,073
the Beatles were already the biggest thing

267
00:09:20,073 --> 00:09:20,855
in England.

268
00:09:21,035 --> 00:09:23,206
In December, I Want to Hold Your

269
00:09:23,206 --> 00:09:24,902
Hand came out in America.

270
00:09:25,082 --> 00:09:27,485
Within weeks, it was everywhere.

271
00:09:27,665 --> 00:09:30,569
By the time a ten-year-old turned eleven,

272
00:09:30,569 --> 00:09:32,553
the whole world had changed.

273
00:09:32,903 --> 00:09:35,097
The eighth reason was family.

274
00:09:35,277 --> 00:09:38,095
Most ten-year-olds in sixty-three sat down to

275
00:09:38,095 --> 00:09:40,787
dinner with their whole family every single

276
00:09:40,787 --> 00:09:41,163
night.

277
00:09:41,343 --> 00:09:43,619
Grandparents often lived nearby.

278
00:09:43,799 --> 00:09:46,932
Aunts, uncles and cousins came for Sunday

279
00:09:46,932 --> 00:09:47,467
dinner.

280
00:09:47,647 --> 00:09:49,025
Holidays were huge,

281
00:09:49,025 --> 00:09:50,257
loud and crowded,

282
00:09:50,257 --> 00:09:52,721
with a kids' table in the kitchen.

283
00:09:53,071 --> 00:09:55,632
Families took road trips together,

284
00:09:55,632 --> 00:09:57,214
in the station wagon,

285
00:09:57,214 --> 00:10:00,153
without seat belts or air conditioning.

286
00:10:00,333 --> 00:10:02,808
They stopped at roadside diners,

287
00:10:02,808 --> 00:10:04,897
motels with swimming pools,

288
00:10:04,897 --> 00:10:07,914
and tourist traps with signs for miles.

289
00:10:08,094 --> 00:10:10,942
Kids counted license plates and asked,

290
00:10:10,942 --> 00:10:11,991
over and over,

291
00:10:11,991 --> 00:10:13,191
how much longer.

292
00:10:13,541 --> 00:10:16,002
At home, there were family rituals.

293
00:10:16,182 --> 00:10:17,934
Friday night was fish,

294
00:10:17,934 --> 00:10:20,243
or maybe a pizza from the new

295
00:10:20,243 --> 00:10:21,836
pizza place in town.

296
00:10:22,016 --> 00:10:25,232
Saturday night was baths and hair washing.

297
00:10:25,412 --> 00:10:26,888
Sunday was church,

298
00:10:26,888 --> 00:10:29,840
a big dinner and The Wonderful World

299
00:10:29,840 --> 00:10:30,578
of Color.

300
00:10:30,758 --> 00:10:33,523
These little routines gave kids a sense

301
00:10:33,523 --> 00:10:35,791
of order that many of them still

302
00:10:35,791 --> 00:10:36,145
miss.

303
00:10:36,496 --> 00:10:38,716
The ninth reason was that a little

304
00:10:38,716 --> 00:10:40,153
money went a long way.

305
00:10:40,333 --> 00:10:43,718
A ten-year-old with an allowance of fifty

306
00:10:43,718 --> 00:10:45,534
cents a week was rich.

307
00:10:45,714 --> 00:10:48,744
A comic book cost twelve cents.

308
00:10:48,924 --> 00:10:51,026
A candy bar was a nickel.

309
00:10:51,206 --> 00:10:52,958
A bottle of Coke was a dime,

310
00:10:52,958 --> 00:10:54,836
and you got two cents back for

311
00:10:54,836 --> 00:10:55,524
the bottle.

312
00:10:55,704 --> 00:10:58,096
A movie matinee cost a quarter.

313
00:10:58,447 --> 00:11:00,037
Kids earned money too.

314
00:11:00,217 --> 00:11:02,881
They collected soda bottles from ditches for

315
00:11:02,881 --> 00:11:03,607
the deposit.

316
00:11:03,787 --> 00:11:05,090
They mowed lawns,

317
00:11:05,090 --> 00:11:08,461
shoveled snow, sold lemonade and ran errands

318
00:11:08,461 --> 00:11:09,534
for neighbors.

319
00:11:09,714 --> 00:11:12,440
Many ten-year-olds had a savings account at

320
00:11:12,440 --> 00:11:14,849
the bank, with a little passbook where

321
00:11:14,849 --> 00:11:16,878
the teller stamped each deposit.

322
00:11:17,228 --> 00:11:19,700
And the tenth reason was innocence.

323
00:11:19,880 --> 00:11:21,713
In nineteen sixty-three,

324
00:11:21,713 --> 00:11:24,996
most ten-year-olds believed the world was a

325
00:11:24,996 --> 00:11:25,836
good place.

326
00:11:26,016 --> 00:11:27,506
Grown-ups were in charge,

327
00:11:27,506 --> 00:11:29,471
and they seemed to know what they

328
00:11:29,471 --> 00:11:30,126
were doing.

329
00:11:30,306 --> 00:11:32,257
The president was young,

330
00:11:32,257 --> 00:11:34,370
handsome and full of hope.

331
00:11:34,550 --> 00:11:36,791
America seemed like it could do anything.

332
00:11:37,141 --> 00:11:40,804
That innocence was shaken on November twenty-second,

333
00:11:40,804 --> 00:11:42,284
nineteen sixty-three.

334
00:11:42,464 --> 00:11:45,408
Many ten-year-olds were sitting in class when

335
00:11:45,408 --> 00:11:48,547
the principal's voice came over the loudspeaker.

336
00:11:48,727 --> 00:11:51,653
President Kennedy had been shot in Dallas.

337
00:11:51,833 --> 00:11:52,878
Teachers cried.

338
00:11:53,058 --> 00:11:54,939
Children were sent home early.

339
00:11:55,119 --> 00:11:57,084
For many, it was the first time

340
00:11:57,084 --> 00:11:59,113
they ever saw their fathers cry.

341
00:11:59,463 --> 00:12:00,883
For the next four days,

342
00:12:00,883 --> 00:12:03,723
the whole country watched television together.

343
00:12:03,903 --> 00:12:06,168
The funeral, the riderless horse,

344
00:12:06,168 --> 00:12:09,667
and three-year-old John Kennedy Junior saluting his

345
00:12:09,667 --> 00:12:10,765
father's casket.

346
00:12:10,945 --> 00:12:13,446
Many people say that weekend was the

347
00:12:13,446 --> 00:12:15,043
end of their childhood.

348
00:12:15,223 --> 00:12:16,148
They were ten,

349
00:12:16,148 --> 00:12:18,461
but they never quite felt that safe

350
00:12:18,461 --> 00:12:18,857
again.

351
00:12:19,208 --> 00:12:22,067
And yet, what most people remember about

352
00:12:22,067 --> 00:12:25,140
being ten in sixty-three isn't the sadness.

353
00:12:25,320 --> 00:12:26,470
It's the bikes,

354
00:12:26,470 --> 00:12:28,616
the fireflies, the cartoons,

355
00:12:28,616 --> 00:12:31,683
the astronauts and the long summer days.

356
00:12:31,863 --> 00:12:34,199
It's the feeling that the whole neighborhood

357
00:12:34,199 --> 00:12:36,111
was yours, and that there was always

358
00:12:36,111 --> 00:12:37,226
someone to play with.

359
00:12:37,577 --> 00:12:40,309
If you were ten in nineteen sixty-three,

360
00:12:40,309 --> 00:12:42,836
you're in your early seventies today.

361
00:12:43,016 --> 00:12:44,996
You've seen the moon landing,

362
00:12:44,996 --> 00:12:47,590
the computer age and everything since.

363
00:12:47,770 --> 00:12:49,594
But I'd bet that if you close

364
00:12:49,594 --> 00:12:51,670
your eyes, you can still hear the

365
00:12:51,670 --> 00:12:54,186
screen door slam and your mother calling

366
00:12:54,186 --> 00:12:55,444
you home for dinner.

367
00:12:55,795 --> 00:12:57,757
So tell us in the comments.

368
00:12:57,937 --> 00:13:00,250
How old were you in nineteen sixty-three,

369
00:13:00,250 --> 00:13:01,547
and where did you live?

370
00:13:01,727 --> 00:13:03,421
What was your favorite toy,

371
00:13:03,421 --> 00:13:04,613
your favorite show,

372
00:13:04,613 --> 00:13:06,557
or your favorite summer memory?

373
00:13:06,737 --> 00:13:08,246
We read every comment.

374
00:13:08,426 --> 00:13:10,981
Next time, we're asking what happened to

375
00:13:10,981 --> 00:13:12,130
the soda fountain.

376
00:13:12,310 --> 00:13:14,232
Thank you for spending this time with

377
00:13:14,232 --> 00:13:14,388
me.
